---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import InstanceNote from "../components/api/InstanceNote.astro";
import OptionsTable from "../components/api/OptionsTable.astro";
import PropertiesTable from "../components/api/PropertiesTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Introduction", heading: false },
  { id: "rich", label: "Rich" },
  { id: "javascript", label: "JavaScript" },
  { id: "initialization", label: "Initialization" },
  { id: "options", label: "Options" },
  { id: "methods", label: "Methods" },
  { id: "properties", label: "Properties" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="tooltips">
<PageBody sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          Put a <code>.tooltip</code> child inside the activator to make the bubble.
          The activator does not need a <code>tooltipped</code> class. Tooltips show on
          hover and keyboard focus with CSS alone. Helper classes set only
          placement and the rich variant: <code>top</code> (the default),
          <code>bottom</code>, <code>left</code>, <code>right</code>, and
          <code>rich</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The hover area of a plain tooltip includes the gap to the
          activator. Runtime tooltips support Escape dismissal and reduced
          motion.
        </p>
        <p>
          On touch, a runtime tooltip shows after a half-second long press,
          without <code>enterDelay</code>. It stays for a second and a half after the finger lifts.
          The long press does not press the button or follow the link. A tap,
          or a press that turns into a scroll, shows nothing. With
          <code>openOnClick</code>, a tap counts as the click. It opens and closes
          the tooltip, and the long press is off.
        </p>
        <p>
          A runtime tooltip skips its <code>enterDelay</code> if it opens while
          another tooltip is closing, or within 300 ms after that one's
          <code>exitDelay</code> ends. Moving along a toolbar therefore shows each
          tooltip at once.
        </p>
        <p>
          CSS-only tooltips have no Escape handler. Use them only after you
          verify the applicable WCAG 1.4.13 conditions and dismissal exception.
          Otherwise, use a verified runtime tooltip or persistent supporting text.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tokens follow the
          <a href="https://m3.material.io/components/tooltips/specs">M3 tooltip spec</a>.
          A plain tooltip uses <code>inverse-surface</code> for the container
          and <code>inverse-on-surface</code> for the text, with 4dp corners and
          <code>body-small</code> type. It is at least 24dp tall and at most
          200dp wide, with 4dp vertical and 8dp horizontal padding. It has no
          elevation or caret. It sits 4dp from the activator, and M3 recommends
          placing it above.
        </p>
        <p>
          Inside a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, make the bubble a
          <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>. A <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> is not
          phrasing content, so the parser hoists it out. Icon-only
          buttons still need <code>.circle</code> so the span is not
          treated as a label.
        </p>
        <p>
          <button type="button" class="circle" aria-label="Add" aria-describedby="tip-add-to-album">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
            <span class="tooltip" id="tip-add-to-album">Add to album</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="circle" aria-label="Above" aria-describedby="tip-above">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_upward</span>
            <span class="tooltip" id="tip-above">Above</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="circle" aria-label="Below" aria-describedby="tip-below">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_downward</span>
            <span class="tooltip bottom" id="tip-below">Below</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="circle" aria-label="Start" aria-describedby="tip-start">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_back</span>
            <span class="tooltip left" id="tip-start">Start</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="circle" aria-label="End" aria-describedby="tip-end">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_forward</span>
            <span class="tooltip right" id="tip-end">End</span>
          </button>
        </p>
<Code code={`
<button type="button" class="circle" aria-label="Add" aria-describedby="tip-add-to-album">
  <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
  <span class="tooltip" id="tip-add-to-album">Add to album</span>
</button>

<button type="button" class="circle" aria-label="Below" aria-describedby="tip-below">
  <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">arrow_downward</span>
  <span class="tooltip bottom" id="tip-below">Below</span>
</button>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.rich}>
        <p>
          Add <code>rich</code> for a rich
          tooltip with a <code>surface</code> container, elevation 2, 12dp
          corners, and a 320dp maximum width. Inside it, a heading is the title
          (<code>title-small</code>), a <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> is
          supporting text (<code>body-medium</code>), and a trailing
          <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> holds the action. Rich bubbles
          receive pointer events, so the user can click the action.
        </p>
        <div>
          <button type="button" class="tonal">Why this is saved</button>
          <div class="tooltip rich bottom">
            <h3>Saved offline</h3>
            <p>This stop is stored on the device so it still opens without a signal.</p>
            <nav>
              <button type="button" class="text">Got it</button>
            </nav>
          </div>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<div>
  <button type="button" class="tonal">Why this is saved</button>
  <div class="tooltip rich bottom">
    <h3>Saved offline</h3>
    <p>This stop is stored on the device so it still opens without a signal.</p>
    <nav>
      <button type="button" class="text">Got it</button>
    </nav>
  </div>
</div>
`} />
        <p>
          A rich tooltip with an action cannot go inside a
          <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, because that would nest buttons. Put the
          bubble next to the control and wrap both in a parent.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.javascript}>
        <p>
          To use the JS plugin, add
          <code>tooltipped</code> to the activator.
          <code>data-tooltip</code> holds the text, and
          <code>data-position</code> is <code>top</code>,
          <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, or
          <code>left</code>. The plugin handles
          <code>data-tooltip</code>, delayed show and hide, and
          keeping the bubble inside the viewport. CSS-only tooltips do not need AutoInit.
          <code>AutoInit()</code> starts every
          <code>.tooltipped</code> except those marked
          <code>no-autoinit</code>. The generated element gets
          <code>.tooltip</code> (plus <code>.rich</code> when <code>tooltipId</code> is set).
        </p>
        <p>
          In browsers with CSS anchor positioning, the plugin anchors the bubble to its activator instead of measuring. The bubble stays beside the activator while the page or a container scrolls, and flips to the opposite side at the viewport edge. An anchored tooltip fades in without sliding, because script cannot tell which side it flipped to. The plugin adds <code>transitionMovement</code> to the gap instead. Other browsers keep the measured, viewport-clamped position.
        </p>
        <p class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <button type="button" class="tooltipped" data-position="bottom" data-tooltip="I am a tooltip">Bottom</button>
          <button type="button" class="tooltipped" data-position="top" data-tooltip="I am a tooltip">Top</button>
          <button type="button" class="tooltipped" data-position="left" data-tooltip="I am a tooltip">Left</button>
          <button type="button" class="tooltipped" data-position="right" data-tooltip="I am a tooltip">Right</button>
        </p>
<Code code={`
<button type="button" class="tooltipped" data-position="bottom" data-tooltip="I am a tooltip">
  Hover me
</button>
`} />
        <p>
          For HTML content, point <code>data-tooltip-id</code> at an element.
          The plugin moves that element into the tooltip and marks the
          bubble <code>rich</code>. Leave <code>data-tooltip</code> off
          so the plugin keeps the HTML. There is no <code>data-html</code>
          attribute and no <code>unsafeHTML</code> option.
        </p>
        <p>
          <button type="button" class="tooltipped" data-position="bottom" data-tooltip-id="tooltip-content">With HTML</button>
        </p>
        <div id="tooltip-content" style="display: none;">
          <h3>Chart</h3>
          <p>This is a tooltip with a <a href="https://github.com">link</a>
          and a <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">insert_chart</span>.</p>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<button type="button" class="tooltipped"
        data-position="bottom" data-tooltip-id="tooltip-content">
  With HTML
</button>
<div id="tooltip-content" hidden>
  <h3>Chart</h3>
  <p>This is a tooltip with a <a href="https://github.com">link</a>.</p>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.initialization}>
        <p>
          The IIFE bundle exposes <code>Expressive.Tooltip</code>.
          Call <code>init</code> yourself when you need options other
          than the defaults, or let
          <code>Expressive.AutoInit()</code> start every
          <code>.tooltipped</code>.
        </p>
<Code code={`
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  const elems = document.querySelectorAll('.tooltipped');
  const instances = Expressive.Tooltip.init(elems, {
    enterDelay: 200
  });
});
`} />
        <p>You can also pass per-instance options through AutoInit:</p>
<Code code={`
Expressive.AutoInit(document.body, {
  Tooltip: { enterDelay: 200 }
});
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.options}>
        <p>
          Pass these to the constructor or <code>init</code>.
          <code>data-tooltip</code>, <code>data-position</code>, and
          <code>data-tooltip-id</code> on the element
          override these defaults. The plugin reads the attributes again each
          time the tooltip opens, so a later change takes effect on the
          next show.
        </p>
        <OptionsTable>            <tr>
              <td><code>exitDelay</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>200</code></td>
              <td>Delay before the tooltip disappears, in milliseconds.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>enterDelay</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>0</code></td>
              <td>Delay before the tooltip appears, in milliseconds. Skipped while a tooltip is closing and for 300 ms after its <code>exitDelay</code> ends.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>tooltipId</code></td>
              <td>String</td>
              <td>—</td>
              <td>Id of an element used as the tooltip body. Set by <code>data-tooltip-id</code>. Marks the bubble <code>rich</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>text</code></td>
              <td>String</td>
              <td><code>''</code></td>
              <td>Plain-text content. Set by <code>data-tooltip</code>. Ignored when <code>tooltipId</code> is set.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>margin</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>4</code></td>
              <td>Distance from the activator, in pixels, not counting <code>transitionMovement</code>. The M3 value is 4dp.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>inDuration</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>250</code></td>
              <td>Enter transition duration, in milliseconds.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>opacity</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>1</code></td>
              <td>Opacity of the tooltip when shown.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>outDuration</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>200</code></td>
              <td>Exit transition duration, in milliseconds.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>position</code></td>
              <td>String</td>
              <td><code>'bottom'</code></td>
              <td>Direction: <code>'top'</code>, <code>'right'</code>, <code>'bottom'</code>, or <code>'left'</code>. Set by <code>data-position</code>. CSS-only tooltips default to above.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>transitionMovement</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>10</code></td>
              <td>How far the tooltip moves during its transition, in pixels.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>openOnClick</code></td>
              <td>Boolean</td>
              <td><code>false</code></td>
              <td>Open on a click instead of hover and focus, at once, without <code>enterDelay</code>. A second click, Escape, a click elsewhere or focus moving to another element closes it at once. A click inside the tooltip does not. Keyboard users open it with Enter or Space, so make the trigger a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>.</td>
            </tr>
  </OptionsTable>  </Section>
  <Section {...S.methods}>
        <InstanceNote plugin="Tooltip" />

        <p>
          <button type="button" class="tooltipped" id="tooltip-method-demo" data-position="bottom" data-tooltip="I am a tooltip">Hover me</button>
          <button type="button" class="tonal" id="tooltip-open">Open</button>
          <button type="button" class="tonal" id="tooltip-close">Close</button>
        </p>

        <h5 class="light">.open();</h5>
        <p>
          <code>open()</code> and <code>open(true)</code> show the tooltip after
          its enter delay, with no hover or focus needed. <code>open(false)</code>
          uses the automatic behavior, so the activator must be hovered or focused when the delay ends.
          With <code>openOnClick</code>, a click elsewhere also closes a tooltip
          opened this way.
        </p>
<Code code={`
instance.open();
`} />

        <h5 class="light">.close();</h5>
        <p>Hide the tooltip.</p>
<Code code={`
instance.close();
`} />

        <h5 class="light">.destroy();</h5>
        <p>Destroy the plugin instance and remove its generated tooltip and event handlers. Rich content returns to its authored position and presentation state. The method keeps existing description associations, so you can initialize the same content again.</p>
<Code code={`
instance.destroy();
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.properties}>
        <PropertiesTable el={"The DOM element the plugin was initialized with."}>            <tr>
              <td><code>isOpen</code></td>
              <td>Boolean</td>
              <td>Whether the tooltip is open.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>isHovered</code></td>
              <td>Boolean</td>
              <td>Whether the activator is hovered.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>isFocused</code></td>
              <td>Boolean</td>
              <td>Whether the activator is focused via the keyboard.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>tooltipEl</code></td>
              <td>Element</td>
              <td>The generated tooltip element.</td>
            </tr>
  </PropertiesTable>  </Section>
</PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
